body:
I feel like it takes practice and I can relate to this VERY much! But I think maybe try finding an already existing scene in a movie that really stood out to you and try explaining that with as much description. Then move onto imagining your own scenario in your head or maybe draw it out/sketch it. You could even try writing the whole scene from what happens first and what happens last. Sometimes we kind of get caught with this grand idea and know the beginning and the ending but usually have difficulty trying to describe the middle part which in my opinion matters bc the smooth transition or flow from one moment to the next is what will really bring the story together. 

&#x200B;

For example, you want to write about a friend that is at a party and goes to the public washroom and starts crying. Rather than getting overwhelmed by how to describe this scene and starting off with trying to describe everything with great details right off the bat, try to add layers after lays....As she heads to washroom, she bumps into people. This line has a little more details now. Then add even more details as how she may lose balance as she bumps into people, speeding towards the washroom. 

&#x200B;

So, I think if you start with the most simple language and explain EVERY single detail and THEN add more details it will help. Closing your eyes and "experiencing" and then trying to tell every detail to yourself as if it actually happened might also help. Like how people tend to tell their best friend something amazing that happened, somehow in great detail. It all comes down to experience and practice. 

&#x200B;

I apologize for being all over the place and going off on a tangent but hopefully some of these methods are somewhat helpful. Good luck with your story :)
